    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d203">

                <pb facs="0276" n="266" sameAs="frus:frus1948v08/pg266"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.20 Mission/7–1148: Telegram</note>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1948-07-11T12:00:00+09:00">July 11,
                        1948—noon.</date><lb/>[Received July 11—4:22 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1261. With the authority given me by the President to supervise and coordinate
                    the activities of US agencies in China, I have sought to harmonize the
                    conflicting personalities and interests of Admiral Badger, ComNavWesPac, General
                    Barr, Chief AAG, and Admiral Thurber,<anchor n="62" xml:id="d203-f1"/> Chief
                    NAG. My efforts have not resulted in complete success and I feel I should bring
                    the situation to your personal attention for such remedial action if any you may
                    deem desirable. There is not only a conflict of personalities but also a
                    conflicting concept of the problem.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>I bring this situation to your personal attention in the belief that if the
                    interests of the United States are not to suffer the system of unified command
                    that worked so well during the war should be instituted immediately in the
                    Advisory Group in China. The Army interest being the predominant one, I strongly
                    recommend that Barr be <pb facs="0277" n="267" xml:id="pg267"/> made Chief of a
                    reorganized Military Advisory Group comprised of Ground Forces, Air and Navy
                    sections. Such reorganizations should assure coordinated effort in the Advisory
                    Group and at the same time provide forum with representation from each service
                    to which Badger as ComNavWesPac could submit his recommendations.<anchor n="63"
                        xml:id="d203-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d203-f1" n="62" xml:id="d203-f1-content">
                <p>Rear Adm. Harry R. Thurber had succeeded Rear Adm. S. S. Murray as Senior Member
                    of the Naval Advisory Group Survey Board.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d203-f2" n="63" xml:id="d203-f2-content">
                <p>Rear Adm. Francis P. Old was appointed to succeed Rear Adm. Thurber in August
                    1948.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
